    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d108">

                <pb facs="0091" n="83" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg83"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">751G.92/4: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Thailand</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grant</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Bangkok</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-08-21T17:00:00+07:00">August 21, 1940—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 21—10:47 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>59. The Prime Minister, according to the most reliable and confidential
                    information, communicated a message last evening to the French Minister<anchor
                        n="41" xml:id="d108-f1"/> requesting him to telegraph the Vichy Government
                    and urge that all of the provinces ceded by Thailand to France in Indochina be
                    omitted from any settlement arising from capitulation of the Vichy Government to
                    any demands relating to Indochina. The Prime Minister also indicated that the
                    Japanese were bringing pressure to bear on his Government in connection with the
                    Indochina matter and that the reasons Japan had not already taken direct action
                    were, <pb facs="0092" n="84" xml:id="pg84"/> (1) the uncertain internal
                    situation in Japan, (2) the attitude of Germany and (3) the attitude of the
                    United States.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grant</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
